(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-3-pulm-bronchitis-cap-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Acute Bronchitis & Community-Acquired Pneumonia Quiz 2 — Pharmacology I Exam 3","category":"Pharmacology I","exam":"Exam 3","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"How does treatment of community-acquired pneumonia in the intensive care unit differ from treatment on the ward?","choices":["The beta-lactam is given by mouth","Every drug is started intravenously","Antibiotics wait for culture results","Staphylococcal coverage is mandatory"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Intensive care unit treatment uses the same regimens, but all treatment begins intravenously, and coverage for methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus is considered if warranted. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"A patient is admitted to a medical ward, outside the intensive care unit, with community-acquired pneumonia. Which regimen fits?","choices":["Azithromycin by mouth alone","Cefepime plus tobramycin","Doxycycline by mouth alone","Ceftriaxone plus azithromycin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Non-intensive care inpatients get an intravenous beta-lactam (cefotaxime, ceftriaxone or high-dose ampicillin) plus an intravenous or oral macrolide. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Which patients does chronic bronchitis primarily affect?","choices":["Children exposed to a day care center","Those with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease","Patients receiving mechanical ventilation","Previously healthy adults of every age"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Acute bronchitis affects all ages, while chronic bronchitis primarily affects patients with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"In acute bronchitis, symptoms that persist beyond five days should prompt consideration of what?","choices":["Chronic bronchitis","Antihistamine therapy","Bacterial involvement","A resistant virus"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Routine antibiotics are not recommended, but persistent symptoms beyond 5 days should prompt consideration of bacterial involvement. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Which drug group is part of the supportive care for acute bronchitis?","choices":["Antihistamines","Antitussives","Sympathomimetics","Macrolides"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Supportive care for acute bronchitis is analgesics, antipyretics, antitussives and fluids. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Why is the sputum gram stain and culture in pneumonia considered controversial?","choices":["It must follow the first antibiotic","It cannot be done in hospitalized patients","It replaces the need for blood cultures","Expectorated samples are often contaminated"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Sputum gram stain and culture is controversial: expectorated sputum is frequently contaminated and the responsible pathogen is not always identified. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"A previously healthy adult has had a cough for three days after a cold and is diagnosed with acute bronchitis. What is the appropriate antibiotic plan?","choices":["Azithromycin","Doxycycline","No antibiotic","Levofloxacin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Acute bronchitis is generally self-limited and treated with symptomatic, supportive care only; routine antibiotics are not recommended and are a common unnecessary prescription. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"A patient admitted to a ward with community-acquired pneumonia has a beta-lactam allergy. Which drug can be used in place of the beta-lactam-based regimen?","choices":["Cefotaxime","Ampicillin","Levofloxacin","Doxycycline"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. For hospitalized patients, a respiratory fluoroquinolone replaces the intravenous beta-lactam plus macrolide regimen when there is a beta-lactam allergy. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"An outpatient with community-acquired pneumonia has risk factors for resistant Streptococcus pneumoniae. Which beta-lactam is paired with a macrolide?","choices":["Cefuroxime","Cefepime","Ertapenem","Ceftazidime"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. With resistance risk factors, a beta-lactam (amoxicillin with or without clavulanic acid, cefuroxime or cefpodoxime) is combined with a macrolide. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"For a patient hospitalized with community-acquired pneumonia, when should blood and sputum cultures be obtained?","choices":["Before antibiotics are started","After 48 hours of antibiotics","When empiric therapy fails","At discharge from the hospital"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Blood and sputum cultures are obtained for hospitalized patients, and they are drawn prior to antibiotics. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"Which medication history is a risk factor for penicillin-resistant Streptococcus pneumoniae?","choices":["A known allergy to beta-lactams","A beta-lactam taken 2 years ago","Daily antitussive use for a cough","A beta-lactam within the past 3 months"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A beta-lactam within the past 3 months is listed with age over 65, alcoholism, immunosuppression, comorbidities and day care exposure. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 70-year-old outpatient with community-acquired pneumonia took amoxicillin 2 months ago. Which single drug is an appropriate regimen?","choices":["Azithromycin","Doxycycline","Minocycline","Levofloxacin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Age over 65 and a beta-lactam within 3 months are risk factors for resistant Streptococcus pneumoniae, so the options are beta-lactam plus macrolide or a respiratory fluoroquinolone alone. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Which pathogen is listed for intensive care unit pneumonia but not for outpatient pneumonia?","choices":["Staphylococcus aureus","Streptococcus pneumoniae","Haemophilus influenzae","Moraxella catarrhalis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Staphylococcus aureus appears on the intensive care unit list (with Legionella and gram-negative bacilli), not on the outpatient list. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 12"}]};
